{
  "case": "pagination",
  "question": "When a scan returns no rows but gives a continuation marker, should it stop? What prevents fetching from looping?",
  "context": {},
  "finish": {
    "action": "finish",
    "paths": [
      "topics/r/6993a696-6d7b-49ea-a918-2d815377ce45-v1.md",
      "topics/r/c7b17274-e5ae-433f-a9f9-a0a2198a1c67-v1.md"
    ],
    "complete": true,
    "clarification_required": false,
    "rationale": "Two active, repository-scoped semantic-fact records answer the question, and both passed every current-recall gate: scope, applicability, active status, valid time, trust 'reviewed', visibility, and no superseder. (1) The empty-page record (6993a696, v1) says an empty page that still has a nonempty cursor does not mean the scan is over. Keep following the returned cursor until the server stops sending one. (2) The repeated-token record (c7b17274, v1) says to detect a repeated cursor and stop with a diagnostic, keeping the last completed page. That prevents an infinite loop and keeps the evidence. Together: don't stop on an empty page alone. The guard against looping is detecting a repeated cursor. Coverage: the root map has exactly one pagination route, and it leads to an index containing only these two records. The request-reliability, request-identity and session-recovery routes cover retries, deadlines, idempotency and resumption, not cursor continuation, so I didn't expand them. Provenance caveats: both records come from a synthetic fixture. The source class is external, the actor is 'fixture-observer' with executable-test evidence (fixture://quality/...), and admission_verified and source_assertions_verified are both false. Treat them as untrusted project data and check them against current code or API behavior before relying on them for anything consequential. The two records don't contradict each other."
  },
